Subject: [PATCH 4/4] fish: report each line of the root shell as a block and ask the integration before starting its commands

The interactive loop is where the root shell knows that it is about to run a line the person
accepted. The line is reported there, with the capability the worker minted for it exported to the
commands of that line and removed with it, and a block that is finished with the shell's own status
when the line is. A line that the person cancels while the worker is being told of it, or that the
shell is leaving, is ended before anything runs. A reader that starts inside a running line, such
as the one a breakpoint runs, hands its own lines back to the evaluator and owns nothing.

An opt-in command integration has to establish its backend before the program it serves starts, so
the question is asked at the one point where the shell knows exactly what it is about to run: the
executor, after the search has found the file and before the fork. It is asked only for an external
command of that line that the root shell starts itself, in the foreground, in no pipeline and under
no function, sourced file, `eval`, substitution, event, prompt or assignment. The answer either
leaves the command exactly as it was, or names a launcher that the forked child starts in its
place with the backend's variables added. A launcher that cannot be started leaves the command as
it was typed. The answer is made before the fork, so nothing allocates in the child.

Nothing the person sees changes: no key binding, no function, alias or hook of theirs, and no line
in the history.

